ls":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Autolus Therapeutics Plc, London W12 7FP, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Ferrari M","lastName":"Ferrari","firstName":"Mathieu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Autolus Therapeutics Plc, London W12 7FP, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Pule M","lastName":"Pule","firstName":"Martin","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Autolus Therapeutics Plc, London W12 7FP, UK; Developmental Biology and Cancer Department, The Cancer Institute, Paul O'Gorman Building, University College London, London WC1E 6DD, UK. Electronic address: m.pule@autolus.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4854-5221"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-2769-9491"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"34","journalIssueId":4254667,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mol Ther","title":"Molecular therapy : the journal of the American Society of Gene Therapy","essn":"1525-0024","issn":"1525-0016","nlmid":"100890581","isoabbreviation":"Mol Ther"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"5175-5189","abstractText":"Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T cells require an extracellular targeting domain for antigen specificity, typically an scFv. VHH antibodies are emerging as alternative CAR binding regions due to their reduced size, improved stability, and CDR3 loop architecture. Herein, we generated and compared VHH and scFv antibodies targeting CD123, via immunized phage display libraries, selecting ten antibody pairs based on comparable domain targeting and kinetic profiles (K<sub>D</sub> range M 10<sup>-9</sup>-10<sup>-11</sup>). The VHH antibody fragments showed improved stability (Tm<sub>50</sub> Δ7.85°C), reduced aggregation, and a favorable surface charge. CARs incorporating a VHH demonstrated higher IL2 and IFNγ secretion than scFv-derived CARs during in vitro analysis and substantially enhanced survival and decreased tumor burden in a xenograft murine model of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). This comprehensive comparison of the two most adopted antibody classes provides a rationale for the selection of VHH as preferred CAR binding domains.","affiliation":"Autolus Therapeutics Plc, London W12 7FP, UK.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"T-Lymphocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunotherapy, Adoptive","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Xenograft Model Antitumor Assays"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Interleukin-3 Receptor alpha Subunit","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Leukemia, Myeloid, Acute","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"IM","qualifierName":"immunology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Single-Chain Antibodies","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":